<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emir</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as emirs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An emir is an Arabic title meaning commander or prince, used for military leaders, governors and rulers in Islamic states. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> emirs were the senior military officers, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> powerful emirs dominated late politics, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> emirs governed the constituent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emir.jpg" alt="Emir" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shuja_Shah_Durrani_and_Men_in_his_decorated_palace_Wellcome_V0050531.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
